{
  "duration": 98.847,
  "fps": 30,
  "totalFrames": 2980,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 6.08,
      "text": "In 1904, the Olympic marathon was so disastrous that the guy who finished first wrote a car,"
    },
    {
      "start": 6.7,
      "end": 8.56,
      "text": "and the real winner ran on rat poison."
    },
    {
      "start": 9.58,
      "end": 16.34,
      "text": "St. Louis wanted the grandest race in history across nearly 25 miles of dusty hills in 90-degree heat."
    },
    {
      "start": 16.8,
      "end": 21.6,
      "text": "To test human dehydration, the organizer put almost no water on the entire course."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.32,
      "end": 29.28,
      "text": "Worse, officials drove noisy cars right in front of the pack blasting thick clouds of red dust into every runner's lungs."
    },
    {
      "start": 29.84,
      "end": 35.16,
      "text": "A Cuban mailman showed up in dress trousers, had his pants snipped into shorts at the starting line,"
    },
    {
      "start": 35.5,
      "end": 40.04,
      "text": "eight rotten orchard apples, took a roadside nap, and still placed forth."
    },
    {
      "start": 40.76,
      "end": 44.42,
      "text": "One barefoot runner got chased a mile off course by wild dogs."
    },
    {
      "start": 45.16,
      "end": 50.48,
      "text": "Meanwhile, American Fred Lors rode 11 miles in a car, waving at exhausted runners,"
    },
    {
      "start": 50.96,
      "end": 54.0,
      "text": "then jogged across the finish line, until officials caught him."
    },
    {
      "start": 54.44,
      "end": 56.08,
      "text": "That left Thomas Hicks in first."
    },
    {
      "start": 56.08,
      "end": 61.84,
      "text": "When he began hallucinating, his trainers refused him water, and instead fed him raw egg whites,"
    },
    {
      "start": 62.16,
      "end": 64.18,
      "text": "brandy, and strict nine rat poison."
    },
    {
      "start": 64.84,
      "end": 69.66,
      "text": "Two men literally carried him over the finish line while his legs kept pumping in mid-air."
    },
    {
      "start": 70.2,
      "end": 71.48,
      "text": "Luckily, nobody died."
    },
    {
      "start": 72.06,
      "end": 77.62,
      "text": "Unluckily, over half the runner's quit, and his winning time is still the slowest in Olympic history."
    },
    {
      "start": 78.08,
      "end": 82.58,
      "text": "Running in 90-degree heat without water is like flooring a car with an empty radiator."
    },
    {
      "start": 82.58,
      "end": 86.68,
      "text": "Your blood thickens, your body overheats, and your engine seizes."
    },
    {
      "start": 87.32,
      "end": 92.24,
      "text": "The committee nearly banned the marathon forever, instead making regular water stations mandatory."
    },
    {
      "start": 93.02,
      "end": 98.24,
      "text": "As for Thomas Hicks, he took his gold medal, collapsed, and immediately retired from running forever."
    }
  ],
